---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import VisionTokenCalculator from '../../components/VisionTokenCalculator.jsx';
---
<ToolLayout title="Vision Model Image Token Calculator" description="Roughly estimate how many tokens an image will cost when sent to a vision-capable AI model.">
  <VisionTokenCalculator client:load slot="tool" />

  <h2>Why image token cost isn't obvious from file size</h2>
  <p>Unlike text, an image's token cost depends on its pixel dimensions and how the provider tiles/processes it internally, not its file size on disk — a small compressed JPEG and a large uncompressed PNG of the same pixel dimensions typically cost the same in tokens despite very different file sizes.</p>

  <h2>How to use this calculator</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Enter the image's pixel width and height.</li>
    <li>Enter the number of images and pricing.</li>
    <li>See the estimated token cost.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Common mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Estimating cost from file size</strong> instead of pixel dimensions, which is what actually determines token count for most vision models.</li>
    <li><strong>Not checking provider-specific tiling/detail settings</strong> — some providers offer lower-cost "low detail" processing modes that use fewer tokens for the same image.</li>
    <li><strong>Sending unnecessarily high-resolution images</strong> when a downscaled version would convey the same information at a lower token cost.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Use cases</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Estimating cost before adding image inputs to an AI-powered feature</li>
    <li>Deciding whether to downscale images before sending them to a vision model</li>
    <li>Budgeting for a feature that processes many images through a vision API</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>Is this estimate exact?</strong> It's an approximation — exact image tokenization varies by provider and specific detail/quality settings; check your provider's vision pricing documentation for precise numbers.</p>
</ToolLayout>
